{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kb3DUergi8k9prqZ4Bk2mZ","type":"tool_result","content":"172:## *\"Filter, score, bind — and then a coin flip\"*\n180:## Attention Budget\n211:## 🧭 Soundings\n264:## Why This Chapter Matters\n274:## What You'll Learn\n289:## ⚪ §1 — One Decision, Made Once\n295:### The spine\n343:### And then a coin flip\n353:### The decision is irreversible\n365:## 🔵 §2 — What Makes a Node Feasible\n369:### The mechanism\n377:### Now the part that surprises everyone\n395:### What \"available\" is measured against\n401:### One clause about overhead\n407:### And one clause about making room\n411:### The failure mode, which is the exam-critical half\n425:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 — How the Decision Is Made\n488:## 🔵 §3 — Asking for a Particular Berth\n492:### The direction inverts\n511:### `nodeSelector`\n517:### Node affinity\n536:### The second half of the field name\n542:### The gradient\n580:## 🔵 §4 — When the Berth Refuses You\n584:### The inversion\n603:### The qualification that catches everyone\n611:### The three effects\n651:### Matching\n666:### The taints Kubernetes adds for you\n694:### The mechanism you'd already met\n720:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 — The Two Directions\n784:## 🟡 §5 — Placing Pods Relative to Each Other\n788:### Start with the failure\n796:### Inter-Pod affinity and anti-affinity\n807:### The domain is a variable\n845:### Topology spread constraints\n878:## 🟡 §6 — Overruling the Scheduler, and Replacing It\n882:### `nodeName` — the Pod-level hatch\n898:### The case that makes binding concrete\n910:### `schedulerName` — the cluster-level hatch\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 40]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4dbb537e-21e1-4c5f-9a48-c17071f70862","uuid":"e1e0bbe4-8929-4b50-8e2c-3d414156665c","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:03:14.684Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"172:## *\"Filter, score, bind — and then a coin flip